okay I want to start today with a fact  check along similar lines to others that  we've done in the past of we have been  talking a lot about democratic socialism  versus social democracy and in today's  interview we're actually going to have a  conversation about when we say that we  have a capitalist system what version of  a capitalist system do we actually have  it's not just unfettered total  free-market capitalism and that's going  to be more of a philosophical discussion  but in practice one of the confusions  that has been prevalent among political  pundits and people who talk about  economics is that Denmark and Norway and  Finland and Sweden are not socialist  economies they are mixed economies they  are a well-regulated form of capitalism  that has decided to prioritize social  welfare programs in terms of funding via  taxation in contrast to other countries  like the United States which have not  made those social welfare programs the  same type of priority and there's a  graphic that's been spreading virally  about Norway which number one calls  Norway Democratic Socialist that's one  issue  and number two makes a bunch of specific  statistical claims comparing and  contrasting Norway to the United States  sort of making the case that Norway is a  quote socialist paradise of some kind so  let's look at the graphic and the  comparison and if you're just listening  I'll tell you a bunch of what's on it  and the graphic claims that in Norway's  democratic socialist system there's a  lower poverty rate and a higher life  expectancy than in the United States  that infant mortality is lower and  there's a lower murder rate and there's  less incarceration and a higher GDP per  capita and workers are more protected by  unions the country is happier they have  free health care and free higher  education higher homeownership rate the  minimum wage is a living wage rather  than a poverty wage more vacation more  parental leave and all with only the  slightest increase in tax rate 38.5%  average personal tax rate in Norway  is a 37 percent average personal tax  rate in the United States now if you  have a sense of any of these particular  metrics you might see that graphic and  realize that some of those don't really  sound quite right the idea of an average  tax rate what are we talking about the  median are we talking about an effective  tax rate or a marginal tax rate there's  a lot of stuff in there that doesn't  make immediate sense so when we see a  viral image like this making an argument  that Norway is socialist and is better  off than the United States in always the  first thing you need to do is figure out  whether the numbers it presents are  correct then you need to answer the  question about whether Norway is  actually socialist and only once you do  that can you really make a sort of  qualitative analysis to evaluate the  claim that socialist Norway is better  off than unfettered capitalist United  States so let's look first at some of  the numbers that the the image claims it  says for example that Norway's poverty  rate is 10% and the US poverty rate is  29% I'm not finding that information the  information I found based on the Census  Bureau is that the poverty rate in the  United States just a year and a half ago  was twelve point three percent and that  Norway's poverty rate was four point  three percent so that's sort of a  question mark  the life expectancy numbers cited 81  years in Norway and 79 years in the u.s.  appear to be correct infant mortality is  lower in Norway than in the United  States but I'm finding different numbers  the murder rate is lower in Norway than  in the United States with slightly  different numbers than the ones that are  presented a problematic one in my view  is that average personal tax rate number  at the bottom of the graph the effective  tax rate that people pay is not 37  percent in the United States it heavily  is influenced of course by where you  live and the cost of living of your  state and how that impacts deductions  but generally speaking most of these  indicators are correct in suggesting  that Norway appears to be better off so  once we kind of clarify some of those  numbers we have to get to some of the  more subjective claims that the graphic  makes like for example the graphic  claims that higher education and health  care are quote free in Norway I actually  think that that's sort of a problematic  way to say it they are paid for through  taxation and I think that when we say  it's free  it opens criticism of a very specific  kind which is nothing's actually free  and it starts conversations about  handouts and so on and so forth it would  be better to say countries have taxes  the United States has taxes and Norway  has taxes Norway has different  priorities in terms of how those taxes  are spent and one of those priorities in  Norway is providing health care through  taxes another priority in Norway is  providing public higher education  through taxation I think we're better  off saying that than that they are quote  free because that's both confusing not  completely accurate and also opens up a  number of lines of criticism but then we  get to the most important question is  Norway socialist and the answer is no  Norway is a social democracy in the  Scandinavian capitalism line of thinking  of organizing an economy there's a  common talking point from the right that  if indeed Norway was socialist then it  wouldn't be nearly as well off and in  order to sort of accurately deal with  that talking point from the right the  left shouldn't be incorrectly calling  Norway a socialist country because it's  just not okay and that's a really  important problem with the graphic which  is number one the United States doesn't  actually have totally unfettered  capitalism we prop up industries we  subsidize industries we have almost no  competition in many interests in  industries as I will speak with Denise  Hearn about a little bit later on in the  program and just as importantly Norway  is not a  socialist country there's a common  talking point from the right when you  show them this data comparing Norway in  the United States they will say these  things work in Norway because Norway is  a homogenous white society with few  immigrants so it just works and in the  United States  it wouldn't work because of immigration  and religious a racial diversity rather  this is just wrong  ok Norway has actually the same  foreign-born population percentage as  the United States right around 12 or 13  percent for the last year in which we  have data for both countries so that  talking point is just wrong now it is  true that the United States has more  racial diversity in the way that we  measure race then does Norway but Norway  has a number of internal ethnic groups  groups of different ethnic background  that to us just seem as they as if they  are just all white people the way we  categorize and within Norway these are  actually more significant differences so  it's it's also just not true to say that  Norway is a completely homogeneous  country so the first thing we have to do  any time we see a viral image like this  start to spread is fact check the data  once you've fact check the data evaluate  the labels that are being attached is  the u.s. really totally unfettered  capitalism is Norway actually socialist  once you have accounted for that then we  can make an analysis about which system  appears to be working better for the  people that live within that country you  should be doing this sort of applying  this skeptical eye to every single one  of these memes that spreads over the  internet because some are accurate some  are not and we're only going to be able  to have productive substantive  conversations if we all have an accurate  understanding of what reality is one of  today's sponsors is privacy calm /  Pakman this is a free service that I've  actually been using myself for a while  now and I love it you just take a few  seconds to link your debit card or bank  account to privacy coms mobile app or  browser extension for Firefox and Chrome  and what that does is a  allow you to instantly create virtual  credit cards that are linked to your  bank account so that you're not actually  putting your real debit card number on  the internet and you use these virtual  credit card numbers to buy things online  and with one click of a button you have  the ability to create multiple virtual  cards delete cards freeze and unfreeze  cards you can put a limit on a card so a  company won't be able to charge you more  than you actually want them to and it  protects you from being hacked it  protects you from companies being hacked  which we've been talking about for a  while on the program and it's perfect  for signing up for free trial somewhere  if you don't want your real credit card  number to be exposed and on file and  their app and browser extension let you  autofill the virtual credit card number  into the credit card field when you're  buying things online there are a ton of  different advantages to shopping and  paying bills with a system like this  everything is totally secure and  encrypted and in some situations you can  earn 5% cash back on your purchases it's  all completely free all you have to do  is click the link under this video to  get started and for a limited time  everybody in our audience who signs up  gets $5.00 towards your first purchase  when you sign up using that link below

